| Env var / config | docker-compose / deploy | terraform / infra | CI workflow |
|------------------|-------------------------|-------------------|-------------|
| `CRAWLER_DATABASE_URL` | `x-service.environment`, default `postgres://crawler:crawler@crawler-db:5432/crawler`; `.env.example` host default `…@localhost:4156/crawler` (M6.1) | none — no terraform in repo | none — no CI workflow in repo |
| `CRAWLER_DB_PORT` | `crawler-db` port mapping `${CRAWLER_DB_PORT:-4156}:5432` (M6.1) | none | none |
| `crawler-db` service + `crawler-db-data` volume | new `postgres:18` container (M6.1) | none | none |
| `RULES_API_URL` | `x-service.environment`, default `http://api:8000/api/rules/` (M8.2) | none | none |
| `RULES_REFRESH_SECONDS` | `x-service.environment`, default `600` (M8.2) | none | none |
| `SHELFNET_SEEN_KEY` (internal setting `shelfnet_seen_key`) | removed; Redis hash `shelfnet:seen` becomes orphaned — `docker compose exec redis redis-cli DEL shelfnet:seen` after rollout | none | none |

Rollout order: stop `shelfnet-crawler` and `shelfnet-scraper`; wait until `XPENDING record-raw processors` and
`XPENDING record-processed api` are 0 (v1 messages would otherwise be soft-failed); deploy; `api-migrate`
applies 0002–0004; start `crawler-db` before `shelfnet-crawler`. The first cycle against an empty seen
table re-walks up to `SHELFNET_MAX_WALK_PAGES` pages; re-published records upsert in place.

## Authorization / data access

- [x] Every queryset is documented as public: `/api/records/`, `/api/records/{id}/`, `/api/facets/` stay
      `AllowAny` read-only; the new `/api/rules/` is `AllowAny` read-only (`GET` only) and filters to
      `active=True` for the requested provider — the system is single-tenant with no user-owned data.
- [x] Full surface in scope reviewed: writes to `Rule`, `Provider` and `Record` happen only through the
      Django admin (staff session auth, unchanged) and the stream consumer; no new write endpoint.
- [x] No endpoint exposes another tenant's data — there are no tenants. Rule reasons are business config,
      not personal data. Token auth on `/api/rules/` is deferred until the API is exposed beyond the dev
      host (Out of scope).

## Out of scope

- Removing Redis Streams at `record-raw` / `record-processed` — kept by decision; revisit if producers and
  processor get independent lifecycles (the doc's own re-introduction condition, inverted).
- FX re-valuation ("re-count daily", EUR→USD/GBP) and an FX table — base pair undecided and no consumer
  of non-EUR prices exists; prices stay EUR cents.
- A real machine-translation backend — `StubTranslator` remains; dictionaries cover publisher/city.
- A dedicated shelfnet edition/catalogue endpoint — editions come from the item page; `EditionCache` is the seam.
- Categorical rule conditions (binding, format, audience) — doc's `Rule` shape is numeric `min`/`max`.
- A persisted skip-audit table / admin view of dropped listings — skips are logged only.
- `RecordFilter` / rules / edition cache in `shelfnet-scraper` and `player` — they only stamp `provider`.
- Calling `Bus.reclaim` from `record-processor` (pre-existing gap: pending messages are never reclaimed).
- SPA filters for binding/format/audience and French-language display.
- Auth on `/api/rules/`.
- Migrating the Redis `shelfnet:seen` hash into Postgres.

